# Prose Style

Apply these principles to all prose you write: docs, commit messages, PR descriptions, reports, and replies.

## Core rules

- **Use simple words.** Say "use" not "utilise". Say "show" not "demonstrate".
- **One word for one idea.** Do not write "each and every", "various and sundry", or alternate synonyms inside one paragraph to vary the prose.
- **Short sentences.** ≤ 20 words for instructions and findings. One idea per sentence.
- **Short paragraphs.** One topic per paragraph. ≤ 4 sentences.
- **Active voice.** Write "Run the test", not "The test must be run".
- **No speculative detail.** Do not explain how something might fail in three different ways when the user did not ask. Do not preempt objections the user did not raise.
- **No hedges for their own sake.** "Maybe we could possibly consider" is one word stretched across five. Cut.
- **No emoji, no marketing voice.** This is engineering.

## Why this matters

Verbose agents spiral. They hedge, repeat, restate, qualify — and the *thinking* drifts into the *output* because writing a long answer feels like progress. Tight prose forces tight thinking: if you cannot say it in 20 words, you do not know what you mean yet.

## When to break these rules

- **Reporting a measurement.** Tables and numbers are denser than prose and do not need prose around them.
- **Citing a load-bearing rule.** The hardware-safety prohibition list is a long paragraph on purpose — ambiguity costs a flight.
- **Quoting a constraint the operator wrote verbatim.** Preserve the wording so the agent later can grep it.
- **User explicitly asked for detail.** "Explain in detail" is a permission, not a default.

## What does NOT change

- Code. Style rules govern prose around code, not code itself.
- File structures. A spec still has Sections. A journal still has Append.
- Tables. Tables are prose compressed; they make text tighter, not looser.
- Bash blocks. Commands stay copy-pasteable.

## Anti-patterns to catch yourself doing

| Anti-pattern | Fix |
|---|---|
| "It is worth noting that X" | Just write X. |
| "As previously mentioned" | The reader has the prior turn in context. |
| "In order to" | "To". |
| "A number of" | A number, or "some". |
| "Due to the fact that" | "Because". |
| "At this point in time" | "Now". |
| "In the event that" | "If". |
| "Is able to" | "Can". |
| "It should be noted" | Delete. |
| Restating the question before answering | Answer first. |
| Two synonyms for the same concept in one paragraph | Pick one. |

## Tie-breaker

If you are unsure whether to keep or cut, cut. Detail can always be added on the next turn; bloat cannot be unread.